      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-224685",
      ":question/problem": "Consider two paired random samples $X_{1}, X_{2}, \u2026, X_{10}$ and $Y_{1}, Y_{2}, \u2026, Y_{10}$ of size $n = 10$. It is known that the differences between paired observations $D_{i} = X_{i} - Y_{i}$ are normally distributed with population mean $\u03bc$ and population variance $\u03c3^{2} = 2$.\n\nFill in the missing statements below to construct a $90%$ confidence interval for $\u03bc$.\n\nConsider the random variable\n$\\frac{1}{10}$ {{field-1}}\nwhere $X^(\u2015)$ and $X^(\u2015)$ are the sample means of the given samples.\n\nWe have that\n$\u03c7_{9}^{2}$ {{field-2}}"
